story += [chapter(1,"Introduction"), sec("1.1 Background"), p("Waste sorting is a repeated everyday decision. Packaging, paper products, glass, clothing, batteries, and organic material require different collection or treatment pathways. People may be uncertain about an item's dominant material, especially when the object is damaged, mixed-material, or visually similar to another category. This project investigates a focused technical question: can a supervised image classifier provide a useful first-pass category suggestion through a simple browser-based photo workflow?"), p("EcoSort AI connects an image dataset and a transfer-learning model to an application interface. A user can upload a photo or use a camera to capture a still image. The server preprocesses the image, runs the selected Keras model, and returns ranked category scores. The system is intended as a learning prototype and decision aid, not as a municipal recycling authority."), sec("1.2 Problem statement"), p("A user-facing waste classifier must solve more than model inference. It must make the input workflow understandable, handle supported image formats, preprocess an image consistently with training, load the intended class ordering, return interpretable results, and communicate the limits of a single-label prediction. Camera use also requires permission and a secure browser context. The project therefore treats model training and the surrounding web workflow as one integrated system."), sec("1.3 Report organization"), p("The chapters describe the project objectives and boundaries; technical background; existing and proposed approaches; the dataset; software tools; image preparation; methodology; model architecture and training; application implementation; system architecture; interface screenshots; evaluation; verification planning; deployment; risks; future scope; and conclusion. The final sections provide references and practical appendices."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(2,"Objectives and Scope"), sec("2.1 Project objectives"), *bullet_list(["Prepare a supervised image-classification pipeline for twelve waste categories in the supplied dataset.","Use transfer learning with MobileNetV2 and train a project-specific twelve-class classifier.","Expose model inference through a browser app with image upload and ranked prediction scores.","Provide a camera workflow with visible preview, still-image capture, review, and user-triggered prediction.","Support a responsive mobile experience and package the web shell as an installable PWA where browser support permits.","Record model results, technical constraints, and future evaluation needs without overstating validation performance."]), sec("2.2 Scope"), p("The implemented task is image-level, single-label classification across the twelve configured classes. The output is a ranking of likely known classes, with the top five returned by the server. The application does not perform object detection, segmentation, multi-item localization, material-composition analysis, contamination grading, barcode or recycling-symbol reading, or jurisdiction-specific disposal lookup."), sec("2.3 Intended use and exclusions"), p("The prototype is most appropriate for a clear image with one visible item. Users should treat its output as a suggestion and check official local guidance, particularly for batteries and other hazardous or specially collected materials. The project is not a safety-critical tool, a waste-collection management system, or a substitute for trained inspection."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(3,"Technical Background and Related Work"), sec("3.1 Image classification"), p("Image classification maps an input image to one label from a predefined set. A convolutional neural network learns visual representations through layers that respond to patterns such as edges, textures, shapes, and object parts. In a supervised setting, the training examples have labels and the model parameters are updated to reduce a classification loss. The final softmax layer expresses a normalized distribution over the configured categories. Those scores are model outputs; without calibration analysis, they should not be interpreted as guaranteed probabilities of correctness."), sec("3.2 Transfer learning"), p("Training a deep network from random initialization requires substantial labeled data and compute. Transfer learning starts from a model whose visual features were learned on a large source dataset, then adapts some or all of the representation to a target task. In this project, MobileNetV2 provides the feature-extraction backbone and a new dense classifier is trained for the repository's waste labels. A staged process first trains the new head while the backbone is frozen and then fine-tunes selected backbone layers at a lower learning rate."), sec("3.3 MobileNetV2"), p("MobileNetV2 was designed as an efficient convolutional architecture using inverted residual blocks and linear bottlenecks. Its design targets favorable trade-offs among model capacity, computation, and memory on constrained devices [1]. In EcoSort AI it is used as a pretrained feature extractor on the server, not as an on-device mobile model. The fact that the architecture is mobile-oriented does not mean this application performs offline inference on a phone."), sec("3.4 Application-layer concepts"), p("Flask provides URL routing and request handling for the Python web service [3]. TensorFlow/Keras provides model construction, training, serialization, and inference. The browser handles file selection, camera permission, still capture, and result presentation. A PWA manifest and service worker support installation and caching of the web shell, but the saved neural-network model remains on the server and predictions require connectivity."), sec("3.5 Project-specific gap"), p("The repository documents an end-to-end implementation but does not provide an independent, production-like test set, per-class precision and recall, a confusion matrix, score calibration, or a recorded field evaluation. The report treats those omissions as evaluation boundaries rather than filling them with invented results."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(4,"System Overview"), sec("4.1 Existing approach"), p("Without a visual decision aid, a user relies on prior knowledge, packaging labels, or separate local disposal information. Manual lookup can be slow, and material labels do not necessarily describe the local collection rules. A general-purpose visual classifier can offer a quick category suggestion, but it may be uncertain or outside its training domain."), sec("4.2 Proposed system"), p("EcoSort AI combines a labeled image dataset, a trained Keras model, a Flask inference service, and a responsive browser interface. The front end accepts a photo or an explicitly captured camera image. The backend validates the requested model and image input, transforms the image to the expected input size, executes the model, and returns the leading category candidates. The UI shows the top result and ranked alternatives."), FlowDiagram(["Labeled images\n12 classes","Prepare and\nsplit data","Train and\nevaluate model","Flask inference\nservice","Upload / camera\nweb interface"]), sec("4.3 Functional requirements"), *bullet_list(["Display available saved models and allow a valid model selection.","Accept supported image file formats and reject missing or unsupported uploads.","Capture a camera still, show it for review, and submit only after the user requests prediction.","Return a top label and up to five ranked model scores.","Show status and error feedback for camera and prediction actions.","Adapt the layout to mobile-sized screens and expose install metadata for supported browsers."]), sec("4.4 Non-functional requirements"), p("The interface should be understandable, responsive, and usable with current browsers. Prediction latency depends on the hosting CPU, memory, model loading, and network. Camera access requires a secure context such as localhost or HTTPS. For public use, service reliability, privacy communication, rate limiting, monitoring, and resource sizing require additional engineering."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(5,"Dataset Description"), p("The repository contains a folder-based image dataset, with class names used as labels. The saved model metadata records twelve categories and a total of 15,515 images. The counts below are read from models/metadata.json and sum to the recorded total."), sec("5.1 Class counts"), make_table([["Class","Images","Share"],["Battery","945","6.1%"],["Biological","985","6.3%"],["Brown glass","607","3.9%"],["Cardboard","891","5.7%"],["Clothes","5,325","34.3%"],["Green glass","629","4.1%"],["Metal","769","5.0%"],["Paper","1,050","6.8%"],["Plastic","865","5.6%"],["Shoes","1,977","12.7%"],["Trash","697","4.5%"],["White glass","775","5.0%"],["Total","15,515","100.0%"]],[58*mm,32*mm,31*mm]), Spacer(1,4*mm), sec("5.2 Class distribution and imbalance"), p("The clothes category accounts for approximately 34.3% of the images, while brown glass and green glass each contain fewer than 650 examples. This imbalance can influence optimization and class-specific performance. The training notebook computes class weights from class counts and passes them into model fitting to increase the contribution of less frequent classes. Weighting is a mitigation strategy; it does not create new examples or guarantee balanced performance."), sec("5.3 Dataset provenance and quality"), p("The repository snapshot does not document a complete dataset source, collection protocol, license, consent basis, deduplication report, or label-review process. These details should be recorded before redistribution or use beyond coursework. The folder structure suggests a single dominant label per image, but the report does not assume that every image has been manually audited. Lighting, camera viewpoint, image background, object condition, and near-duplicate images are relevant sources of variation."), p("The validation split is configured with seed 42 for reproducibility. A fixed seed makes the split repeatable under the same loader and data ordering; it does not by itself establish that the split is stratified or free of near duplicates."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(6,"Tools and Technologies"), make_table([["Component","Use in the project"],["Python 3.11.9","Pinned runtime for compatibility with the TensorFlow deployment environment."],["TensorFlow / Keras","Dataset loading, model definition, transfer learning, training, and inference."],["MobileNetV2","ImageNet-pretrained convolutional backbone used for feature extraction."],["NumPy","Numerical arrays and batch preparation for model prediction."],["Pillow","Image decoding, RGB conversion, and resizing in the prediction service."],["Flask","HTTP routes, form handling, JSON camera endpoint, and template rendering."],["HTML / CSS / JavaScript","Responsive interface, upload controls, camera permission, capture, and results display."],["Gunicorn","WSGI server command configured for hosted execution."],["Render configuration","render.yaml and Python version files describe a hosted web-service setup."],["ReportLab / Matplotlib artifacts","Training plot and project report figures; not part of runtime inference."]],[43*mm,108*mm]), sec("6.1 Runtime compatibility"), p("The repository pins Python 3.11.9 through .python-version and runtime.txt, and render.yaml sets PYTHON_VERSION to 3.11.9. This was added to avoid a deployment selecting a newer Python release for which the configured TensorFlow dependency may not have a compatible distribution. Package constraints in requirements.txt include TensorFlow, NumPy, Matplotlib, Pillow, Flask, and Gunicorn."), sec("6.2 Hardware and software context"), p("Training and inference are resource-dependent. The repository does not specify a fixed GPU, RAM, or CPU requirement, nor does it record a benchmark latency for a named host. The model files are stored under models/. A deployment should be sized and load-tested for its expected concurrency; each worker process may need its own model memory."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(7,"Data Preprocessing"), sec("7.1 Dataset loading and split"), p("The training notebook uses a directory-based TensorFlow image loader. Class folders become label categories. The data is divided into training and validation portions with an 80/20 split and random seed 42. Images are batched in groups of 32, and dataset prefetching is used to overlap input work with model execution."), sec("7.2 Image normalization and sizing"), p("The training input is RGB with spatial dimensions of 224 by 224 pixels. At inference, the Flask service opens the uploaded or captured image with Pillow, converts it to RGB, resizes it to the dimensions specified in metadata, converts it to a TensorFlow image array, and adds a batch dimension before prediction. Matching these dimensions and channel conventions is necessary for compatibility with the trained model."), sec("7.3 Training augmentation"), p("The notebook applies random horizontal flip, rotation up to 0.08, zoom up to 0.12, and contrast adjustment during training. Augmentation exposes the model to modest image variations without changing the user-facing captured file. The validation pipeline is used to monitor generalization and does not serve as a real-world test set."), sec("7.4 Class weighting"), p("The saved metadata records one weight per class, with larger weights assigned to some less frequent classes and a smaller weight for clothes, the largest class. This changes the relative contribution of training examples in the loss. It should be reviewed alongside class-level evaluation because aggregate validation accuracy can hide weak performance on minority labels."), sec("7.5 Data preparation summary"), FlowDiagram(["Read class\nfolders","Split 80 / 20\nseed 42","Resize to\n224 x 224","Batch 32 and\nprefetch","Augment training\nexamples"]), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(8,"Methodology"), sec("8.1 End-to-end methodology"), p("The work proceeds from data inspection to model training, evaluation, application integration, and deployment packaging. Class counts are inspected from metadata; the notebook constructs repeatable train and validation datasets; MobileNetV2 is adapted to the twelve target labels; saved metrics and curves are reviewed; the chosen .keras model and metadata are loaded by Flask; and the browser interface supplies the image workflows."), sec("8.2 Model development sequence"), *bullet_list(["Load labeled images from class directories and create the training and validation partitions.","Apply resizing and training-only augmentation, and calculate class weights.","Load MobileNetV2 with pretrained ImageNet weights and omit its original classification top.","Freeze the backbone and train a task-specific classifier head.","Unfreeze the final thirty backbone layers and fine-tune at a lower learning rate.","Monitor validation behavior, stop or adjust learning rate as configured, and save the best checkpoint.","Record class names, counts, image dimensions, class weights, and validation metrics in metadata."]), sec("8.3 Evaluation approach"), p("The available quantitative evaluation is the saved validation accuracy and validation loss in models/metadata.json. The training-history plot provides epoch-level accuracy and loss curves. The repository does not report an independent held-out test score or a class-by-class confusion analysis, so conclusions are limited to the validation split and saved artifacts."), sec("8.4 Application integration"), p("The web application loads the ordered class labels from metadata, selects an available model file, preprocesses each image according to the same input dimensions, and maps model output indices back to class names. The camera API and upload route both use the same image-prediction function, reducing divergence between those input paths."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(9,"Model Architecture and Training"), sec("9.1 Architecture"), FlowDiagram(["RGB image\n224 x 224","Augmentation\ntraining only","MobileNetV2\nImageNet weights","Global average\npooling + dropout","Dense softmax\n12 categories"]), sec("9.2 Classification head"), p("The model uses MobileNetV2 without its original top layer as a feature extractor. The notebook adds global average pooling, dropout with rate 0.25, and a dense output layer with twelve units and softmax activation. The softmax values are sorted to select the leading candidate classes. The server returns up to five results."), sec("9.3 Optimization and training stages"), make_table([["Training element","Configured approach"],["Initial stage","Train the new classification head while the backbone is frozen, for up to 12 epochs."],["Fine-tuning stage","Unfreeze the final 30 MobileNetV2 layers and train for up to 5 additional epochs."],["Optimizer","Adam; a lower learning rate of 1e-5 is used during fine-tuning."],["Loss","Sparse categorical cross-entropy for integer class labels."],["Early stopping","Monitor validation loss, patience 4, restore best weights."],["Learning-rate scheduling","Reduce learning rate by factor 0.3 after two validation-loss plateaus, with a minimum of 1e-6."],["Checkpoint","Save best_garbage_classifier.keras when validation accuracy improves."]],[43*mm,108*mm]), sec("9.4 Training artifacts"), p("The model directory contains best_garbage_classifier.keras, garbage_classifier_final.keras, metadata.json, and training_history.png. Keeping the class ordering and image size in metadata helps the web application preprocess and interpret model output consistently."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(10,"Web Application Implementation"), sec("10.1 Application structure"), p("The Flask application is defined in app.py. The root route supports GET and POST: GET renders the interface, while POST validates an uploaded file and selected model, calls prediction, and returns the page with the uploaded image, top class, and score list. A separate POST /api/predict-frame route accepts a JSON model name and captured image data URL for camera prediction. A service-worker route serves the static worker script."), sec("10.2 Upload path"), *bullet_list(["The user selects an available .keras model and a supported image file.","The server requires a file, validates its filename extension, and checks that the selected model is in the available model list.","Pillow decodes the image; the prediction helper converts it to RGB and resizes it to metadata dimensions.","TensorFlow runs inference and the server sorts the class scores.","The page shows the submitted image, the top label, its score, and up to five alternatives."]), sec("10.3 Camera path"), *bullet_list(["The browser requests video-only camera access and prefers the rear-facing camera where supported.","The live preview is shown in the camera area.","Capture photo copies the current frame to a canvas and scales it to at most 640 pixels wide.","The captured still is displayed for review. Retake returns to the preview.","Only Predict image sends the captured JPEG data URL and chosen model to /api/predict-frame.","The server decodes the data URL, predicts, and returns the top class and score list as JSON."]), p("This flow is user-triggered still-image classification. The camera preview itself is not continuously sent for inference. The source code stops camera tracks when the user stops the camera or switches away from camera mode."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(11,"System Architecture"), sec("11.1 Component responsibilities"), make_table([["Layer","Responsibilities"],["Browser interface","Image upload, model selection, camera permissions, preview, capture and review controls, results and status messages."],["Flask request layer","Route handling, form and JSON validation, model allow-list checks, request-size limits, and response generation."],["Image preprocessing","Pillow decoding, RGB conversion, and resize to model metadata dimensions."],["Inference service","Cached Keras model loading, probability calculation, top-five sorting, and label mapping."],["Saved artifacts","Keras model files plus JSON metadata containing label order, input size, counts, weights, and metrics."],["Hosting process","Gunicorn serves the Flask WSGI application; Render configuration supplies build and runtime commands."]],[38*mm,113*mm]), sec("11.2 Request flow"), FlowDiagram(["Browser\nfile or still","Flask route\nvalidate input","Pillow\nRGB + resize","Keras model\nclass scores","Browser\nranked output"]), sec("11.3 Data exchange"), p("For uploaded photographs, the browser posts a multipart form to the root route. For camera capture, JavaScript serializes the reviewed still as a JPEG data URL and posts JSON containing the selected model name and image. The camera endpoint verifies that the selected model exists and that the image field is a string before decoding it. The prediction response contains a top_prediction object and a predictions list."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(12,"User Interface Screenshots"), p("The figures below are interface previews reconstructed from the repository's HTML and JavaScript controls. They document the implemented design and workflow but are not screenshots captured from a running Flask/TensorFlow server. The example object image is from the project's dataset."), add_image(BUILD/"slide-08.png", 170*mm, 115*mm), p("Figure 12.1  EcoSort AI upload and camera interface previews. Upload flow includes model selection and category action; camera flow shows capture, review, and prediction states.", "CaptionEco"), sec("12.1 Interface behavior"), p("The page presents two modes, Upload a photo and Scan live. The upload mode includes model selection, a drop zone, and a result panel. Camera mode exposes Start camera, Stop camera, Capture photo, Retake, and Predict image actions. Status messages communicate camera and analysis state. The interface shows alternative score bars so users can see more than the single leading label."), sec("12.2 Mobile and accessibility considerations"), p("The layout uses responsive CSS and the app manifest defines standalone display metadata and an application icon. Native form controls, button labels, status roles, and accessible result regions are present in the template. A browser camera permission can be declined; upload remains an alternative. Installation and camera behavior depend on browser support and a secure origin."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(13,"Results and Discussion"), sec("13.1 Saved validation metrics"), make_table([["Metric","Saved value","Interpretation"],["Validation accuracy","94.68%","Fraction of examples classified correctly on the recorded validation evaluation."],["Validation loss","0.1720","Average loss reported for the selected saved evaluation."],["Output categories","12","Number of configured class labels."],["Dataset size","15,515","Count recorded in the class-count metadata."]],[42*mm,36*mm,73*mm]), Spacer(1,5*mm), sec("13.2 Training history"), add_image(ROOT/"models"/"training_history.png", 165*mm, 90*mm), p("Figure 13.1  Saved training and validation accuracy/loss curves. The plot is a training diagnostic, not an independent test report.", "CaptionEco"), p("The selected model metadata reports validation accuracy 0.9468256 and validation loss 0.1720355. The values in the table are rounded for presentation. The result is a useful project benchmark for the chosen split, but it cannot establish performance across unseen capture conditions or other populations of waste images."), sec("13.3 Interpretation and evidence limits"), *bullet_list(["Overall accuracy can hide errors concentrated in smaller classes such as brown glass or green glass.","The repository does not include a confusion matrix or per-class precision, recall, or F1 values in the saved metadata.","The 80/20 validation split is not documented as a separate, independently curated test set.","The score shown by the app is a model output and has not been documented as calibrated confidence.","No latency, throughput, or deployment load-test result is recorded in the project files."]), sec("13.4 Result conclusion"), p("The project demonstrates a functioning design path from labeled images and transfer learning to a browser-based prediction workflow. The available evidence supports reporting the saved validation metrics and the implemented system features. Broader claims about real-world accuracy, safety, or local recycling outcomes require additional controlled evaluation."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(14,"Verification and Test Plan"), p("The project repository includes application and model code but does not contain a formal test report with recorded execution results. The table below is a recommended verification matrix for the college demonstration and future release; it should not be read as a claim that every test has been executed."), make_table([["Test area","Test case","Expected behavior","Evidence status"],["Upload","Submit a supported image with a valid model.","Show top label, submitted image, and ranked scores.","Recommended; execution not recorded"],["Upload validation","Submit no image or an unsupported extension.","Show a helpful error and do not run inference.","Recommended; execution not recorded"],["Camera permission","Start camera on HTTPS or localhost and allow access.","Display the live preview and camera status.","Recommended; execution not recorded"],["Capture and review","Capture, inspect, then retake.","Show still image; retake restores preview.","Recommended; execution not recorded"],["Prediction action","Predict only after capture and user confirmation.","Send one still and show returned ranked labels.","Recommended; execution not recorded"],["Model selection","Select a missing or invalid model through API request.","Reject the request with a client error.","Code path present; execution not recorded"],["Mobile layout","Open at narrow phone viewport.","Controls remain readable and usable without overlap.","Recommended; execution not recorded"],["Deployment","Install requirements and start Gunicorn with pinned runtime.","Service starts and responds over HTTPS.","Config present; deployment status not verified"]],[25*mm,44*mm,54*mm,28*mm],font=7.1), sec("14.1 Acceptance criteria for a demonstration"), *bullet_list(["The selected model appears in the UI and one known sample image yields a non-empty result.","The camera screen clearly separates preview from captured-image review and prediction.","Unsupported inputs produce a message rather than an unhandled UI failure.","Results are described as model suggestions and the validation/test distinction is stated.","The report, presentation, and demonstration use consistent project facts and metrics."]), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(15,"Deployment and Operation"), sec("15.1 Runtime configuration"), make_table([["Setting","Repository configuration"],["Python runtime","3.11.9 in .python-version, runtime.txt, and render.yaml."],["Build command","pip install -r requirements.txt"],["Start command","gunicorn --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT app:app"],["Application module","app.py; Flask application object is named app."],["Model storage","models/*.keras and models/metadata.json."],["Upload body limit","Flask MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH is 8 MB."],["Camera encoded input","Data URL is rejected when encoded input length exceeds the configured threshold."]],[48*mm,103*mm]), sec("15.2 Local execution"), p("A local setup should use Python 3.11, create an isolated virtual environment, install requirements.txt, and run python app.py. The application binds to localhost and normally serves on port 5000 unless PORT is set. Camera access is expected to work on localhost. A hosted camera workflow requires HTTPS."), sec("15.3 Hosting notes"), p("The repository contains Render-compatible configuration. A successful build depends on the host honoring the pinned Python version and installing compatible TensorFlow packages. The configuration files describe intended deployment; their presence alone does not prove that a public service is currently live. Hosting memory, worker count, startup time, and inference latency should be measured on the selected service plan."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(16,"Privacy, Security, and Operational Risks"), sec("16.1 Current safeguards in code"), *bullet_list(["The upload route checks that a file is supplied and restricts filename extensions.","The selected model name is checked against model files available in the configured models directory.","The Flask request body is limited to 8 MB.","The camera endpoint validates the model selection and image data URL shape and size before decoding.","The project code does not define user accounts, upload libraries, or a database for storing user photos."]), sec("16.2 Remaining production work"), p("Extension checks are not equivalent to full content inspection. A public service should validate actual MIME and decoded image properties, cap dimensions and processing time, apply rate limits, monitor errors, and define a privacy notice. Request and model-loading behavior should be checked under concurrent load. A deployment should avoid exposing debug mode and should use managed secrets if external services are added."), sec("16.3 User-facing risk"), p("A visual label does not establish recyclability, cleanliness, or local disposal eligibility. Battery and hazardous-waste categories require especially careful local instructions. The interface should not imply that the model is infallible or that a confidence score is a verified probability. A low-confidence or unknown response is a recommended improvement."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(17,"Advantages and Limitations"), sec("17.1 Project advantages"), *bullet_list(["The app integrates model inference with both photo upload and camera capture workflows.","Transfer learning reuses pretrained visual features rather than training an entire feature extractor from scratch.","The camera flow gives users a visible preview and review step before an image is submitted.","Class weights address the strongly uneven folder counts during training.","The PWA metadata and responsive layout support a mobile-oriented browser experience.","The model, metadata, training plot, runtime configuration, and application code are organized in the project repository."]), sec("17.2 Current limitations"), *bullet_list(["The classifier predicts one of twelve labels and cannot localize multiple objects in a mixed scene.","Dataset provenance, licensing, collection conditions, and independent label review are not documented in the repository.","Clothes makes up about one third of the images; class weighting cannot guarantee equal class performance.","Only aggregate validation metrics are saved in metadata; per-class metrics and a confusion matrix are absent.","No independent held-out test result, robustness study, or calibration analysis is reported.","The PWA can cache the web shell, but TensorFlow inference remains server-side and needs a network connection.","The app offers material categories, not validated local recycling instructions."]), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(18,"Future Scope"), sec("18.1 Model and dataset improvements"), *bullet_list(["Document source, license, labeling criteria, and collection conditions for each dataset subset.","Create a separate held-out test set with class-wise precision, recall, F1, confusion matrix, and error review.","Collect additional examples for minority categories and varied phones, lighting, backgrounds, viewpoints, and item conditions.","Assess duplicate leakage and consider grouped or stratified splitting where dataset structure allows.","Evaluate score calibration and add an explicit low-confidence or unknown-object outcome.","Explore object detection or segmentation if the intended use includes multiple objects in a frame."]), sec("18.2 Application improvements"), *bullet_list(["Add request rate limiting, image dimension limits, health checks, structured logging, and service monitoring.","Test deployment memory, cold-start time, and inference latency under expected traffic.","Improve accessibility and local-language support with user testing.","Connect disposal guidance only to maintained local rules, with source and jurisdiction displayed.","Evaluate model optimization or on-device inference only after benchmarking supported devices and reviewing privacy implications."]), sec("18.3 Evaluation roadmap"), p("A follow-on evaluation should define the target population and capture conditions before collecting test images. The test set should remain separate from training and model selection. Results should report overall and class-wise metrics, uncertainty or confidence behavior, error examples, and the conditions under which the model should abstain. User studies should assess whether the interface helps people sort correctly rather than merely whether they can operate the app."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(19,"Software Stack at a Glance"), p("EcoSort AI is a small full-stack machine-learning system. Its components are separated by responsibility: Python hosts the model and HTTP service; TensorFlow/Keras defines and executes the neural network; NumPy and Pillow prepare image data; Flask connects HTTP requests to inference; and browser technologies provide the user workflow. Deployment files describe how a compatible runtime can install and start the service."), FlowDiagram(["Browser UI\nHTML / CSS / JS", "HTTP service\nFlask", "Image prep\nPillow / NumPy", "Inference\nTensorFlow / Keras", "Saved model\nMobileNetV2"]), sec("19.1 Component boundaries"), make_table([["Layer","Project technology","Responsibility"],["User interface","HTML, CSS, JavaScript","Image selection, camera preview, still capture, request, and result display."],["Web service","Flask","Routes, request validation, template rendering, and JSON response."],["Image handling","Pillow, NumPy","Decode, convert, resize, and form the model input batch."],["Machine learning","TensorFlow / Keras","Load the saved network and calculate ranked class scores."],["Runtime / deployment","Python 3.11.9, Gunicorn, Render configuration","Dependency installation and hosted WSGI process configuration."]],[32*mm,43*mm,76*mm]), p("The boundaries matter when debugging. A camera permission failure belongs to the browser workflow; a malformed upload is an HTTP/input issue; an import or incompatible wheel is a runtime issue; an incorrect class name may be a metadata-order issue; and a confusing prediction may be a model, image-quality, or domain-shift issue. Treating every failure as an AI problem makes diagnosis harder."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(20,"Python Runtime and Dependency Management"), sec("20.1 Python's role"), p("Python is the common runtime for the training notebook and Flask inference service. In the notebook it orchestrates dataset loading, augmentation, model definition, optimization, validation, and artifact export. In app.py it accepts an image, uses the saved model, and converts model outputs into a response the web interface can display. Python also provides the ecosystem in which TensorFlow, Pillow, NumPy, Flask, and Gunicorn interoperate."), sec("20.2 Runtime pin and the deployment build failure"), p("The repository specifies Python 3.11.9 in .python-version, runtime.txt, and render.yaml. This is important because TensorFlow publishes binary packages for supported Python/platform combinations rather than every possible version. A service that selects a newer Python release may fail while resolving the TensorFlow requirement before the application can start. The project configuration is intended to constrain that runtime; the exact hosted build must still be verified after deployment."), sec("20.3 Requirements and reproducibility"), p("requirements.txt records minimum-version constraints for TensorFlow, NumPy, Matplotlib, Pillow, Flask, and Gunicorn. Minimum constraints permit a range of later releases, so they are not a fully locked dependency solution. For repeatable builds, a future release should record a tested lock file or exact compatible versions, target platform, and installation command. Model and Python compatibility should be validated together."), p("A Python virtual environment isolates packages from other projects. It reduces accidental conflicts but does not fix an unavailable wheel, a wrong interpreter version, insufficient memory, or a model serialization incompatibility. The useful diagnostic sequence is to inspect the selected Python version, platform, package index output, and the first dependency-resolution error."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(21,"TensorFlow and Keras"), sec("21.1 Framework responsibilities"), p("TensorFlow is the numerical and deep-learning framework used by the project. Keras supplies the higher-level model-building API used in the notebook: a pretrained application backbone, augmentation layers, pooling, dropout, a dense classification layer, model compilation, training callbacks, evaluation, and saved-model loading. The inference web service reuses the exported model rather than rebuilding its architecture in the request handler."), sec("21.2 Dataset input and batching"), p("The notebook uses image_dataset_from_directory with integer labels, a 224 by 224 target size, batch size 32, validation_split 0.2, seed 42, and explicit training/validation subsets. Directory names supply class labels. Batching groups examples for efficient accelerator or CPU processing. AUTOTUNE prefetch overlaps data preparation with model execution where possible."), sec("21.3 Saved model and metadata contract"), p("The model artifact and metadata file form a contract. The model expects three-channel image input at the trained spatial resolution and produces twelve scores. metadata.json supplies the class ordering, image size, class counts, weights, and recorded validation values. If model output indices and metadata class_names drift out of alignment, the interface can display a plausible but incorrect label. Both artifacts should therefore be released and tested as one versioned unit."), sec("21.4 Inference behavior"), p("The backend loads a Keras model through a cached loader and calls predict on a one-image batch. It sorts the output and returns up to five candidates. This is a single-label classifier's ranking, not a detection list of every object in a scene. A multi-item photograph may receive one dominant class even though several kinds of material are visible."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(22,"MobileNetV2 Architecture"), sec("22.1 Why this backbone"), p("MobileNetV2 is a convolutional network designed for efficient visual feature extraction. Its inverted residual blocks expand channels, apply depthwise spatial convolution, and project back through a narrow linear bottleneck. Skip connections are used where tensor dimensions allow. The design reduces computation relative to a conventional dense convolution at comparable feature-map sizes [1]."), sec("22.2 Pretrained representation"), p("The notebook requests MobileNetV2 with include_top=False and ImageNet weights. Excluding the original classifier leaves a feature map suitable for a new task-specific head. ImageNet pretraining provides generic visual features learned from a large image collection; the waste dataset then adapts the representation to its twelve directory labels. This is a transfer-learning choice, not evidence that ImageNet contains the project's exact waste categories."), sec("22.3 Input scaling"), p("The application expects 224 by 224 RGB images. MobileNetV2's preprocess_input maps pixel values to the range expected by this architecture. In the notebook, that preprocessing layer is included in the saved model graph. The serving code therefore converts and resizes the image but should not apply an additional incompatible normalization a second time. Consistency between training and serving prevents a subtle but damaging train/serve mismatch."), sec("22.4 Mobile architecture versus mobile execution"), p("Although the backbone is designed for efficiency, the current project runs prediction in the Flask server process. The browser sends the captured or uploaded image over the network. It does not bundle TensorFlow Lite or execute the model locally on the phone. Offline inference would require a separate conversion, on-device integration, performance testing, and model licensing/compatibility review."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(23,"Classifier Head and Transfer-Learning Stages"), FlowDiagram(["224 x 224 RGB", "Augmentation", "MobileNetV2\nfeature maps", "Global average\npooling", "Dropout 0.25\nDense 12", "Softmax\nclass ranking"]), sec("23.1 Classification head"), p("The input passes through the augmentation sequence and MobileNetV2 preprocessing before the pretrained backbone. GlobalAveragePooling2D compresses each feature channel to a scalar, reducing the number of parameters compared with a large flattening layer. Dropout at 0.25 regularizes the new head during training. A Dense layer with twelve softmax outputs maps the learned representation to the configured classes."), sec("23.2 Frozen-backbone training"), p("The first stage freezes the pretrained backbone and trains the newly initialized classification head. This lets the head learn the target label mapping while retaining the pretrained visual features. The notebook compiles with Adam and sparse_categorical_crossentropy, which is appropriate for integer-encoded class labels. Training is configured for up to twelve epochs, subject to early stopping and checkpoint behavior."), sec("23.3 Fine-tuning"), p("The second stage unfreezes the final thirty backbone layers and reduces the learning rate to 1e-5. A small fine-tuning rate helps adapt higher-level visual features without making large destructive updates to the pretrained representation. The notebook allows training up to seventeen total epochs and combines the histories from the two stages for plotting."), p("The training-history figure shows a visible disturbance around the fine-tuning transition. This should be interpreted by inspecting the stage boundary, optimizer state, train/validation curves, and checkpointed best weights. The saved validation metrics identify the selected recorded artifact; they do not remove the need to audit training behavior."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(24,"Augmentation and Class Imbalance"), sec("24.1 Training augmentation"), p("The model applies random horizontal flipping, rotation up to 0.08, zoom up to 0.12, and contrast variation up to 0.1. These transformations are intended to expose the classifier to modest changes in orientation, framing, and illumination. Augmentation is active in training and is designed to be inactive for ordinary inference. It can improve robustness to some variations, but cannot substitute for collecting representative data or correcting mislabeled examples."), sec("24.2 Class weights"), p("The notebook derives a weight for each class from total_images divided by the product of the number of classes and that class's image count. Consequently, classes with fewer examples receive larger per-example weight during training. This changes the contribution to the loss; it does not alter validation examples or add images."), sec("24.3 Interpreting the distribution"), p("Clothes has 5,325 of 15,515 images (about 34.3%), while brown glass has 607 and green glass 629. Shoes is the next largest group at 1,977. Class weighting is a reasonable response to this imbalance, but final behavior must be measured class by class. In particular, aggregate accuracy can conceal poor recall for smaller groups."), sec("24.4 Data hygiene recommendations"), p("Before retraining, document dataset source and permission, identify duplicate or near-duplicate files, review label conventions, and inspect representative errors. If related images originate from the same object, scene, or capture session, group-aware splitting can reduce leakage. Any proposed re-split should be accompanied by newly calculated validation metrics rather than compared as though it were the same experiment."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(25,"Training Controls and Reproducibility"), sec("25.1 Optimization"), p("Adam updates model weights using gradient information with adaptive per-parameter step sizes. The notebook uses sparse categorical cross-entropy as the loss and accuracy as a monitored metric. Class weights are supplied to the fitting process. A training seed helps reproduce the dataset split and related random operations, but exact numerical reproducibility can still depend on TensorFlow, hardware, kernel implementations, and software versions."), sec("25.2 Callbacks"), make_table([["Callback","Configured behavior","Purpose"],["ModelCheckpoint","Save best_garbage_classifier.keras; monitor val_accuracy; save_best_only; mode max","Retain the checkpoint with the strongest monitored validation accuracy."],["EarlyStopping","Monitor val_loss; patience 4; restore_best_weights true","Stop when validation loss does not improve and restore selected weights."],["ReduceLROnPlateau","Monitor val_loss; factor 0.3; patience 2; minimum learning rate 1e-6","Reduce the learning rate when validation loss plateaus."]],[34*mm,63*mm,54*mm]), sec("25.3 Artifact reporting"), p("The notebook evaluates the final model on the validation dataset, saves the Keras artifact and metadata, and plots training history. The metadata reports validation accuracy 0.9468256 and validation loss 0.1720355. These are useful project-reported validation values. The repository snapshot does not include an independently held-out test set, per-class precision/recall, confidence calibration, or repeated-run variance, so those measurements are not claimed here."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(26,"NumPy, Pillow, and Image Preparation"), sec("26.1 Pillow decoding"), p("Pillow opens uploaded image content and provides RGB conversion and resizing. Converting to RGB avoids mode differences such as grayscale or palette images causing an unexpected channel shape. Resizing to metadata's configured 224 by 224 dimensions aligns user images with the network input. Content decoding can still fail for corrupt, truncated, oversized, or unsupported files; a public service should handle those failures cleanly."), sec("26.2 NumPy batch shape"), p("The decoded image is converted to an array and expanded with a batch axis. A single RGB image therefore has a batch shape conceptually equivalent to (1, 224, 224, 3). TensorFlow models commonly accept a batch even when only one image is supplied. The serving pipeline should verify finite values, expected dimensions, and channel count before inference."), sec("26.3 Training/serving parity"), p("Preprocessing parity is crucial: the same color interpretation, resize convention, numeric range, and architecture-specific input scaling should be applied at training and inference. In this project MobileNetV2 preprocessing is part of the saved model. Reapplying a generic 0-to-1 scale outside the model could change the expected input range and lower accuracy. The backend's responsibility is therefore deliberately limited to reliable decode, RGB conversion, resize, array creation, and batch expansion."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(27,"Flask Backend and Prediction API"), sec("27.1 Web routes"), p("Flask is the lightweight Python web framework used to connect browser requests to the model. The root route renders the interface and handles the form workflow. The camera capture path sends a data URL to /api/predict-frame; the endpoint validates the model selection and image payload and returns JSON containing the top prediction and ranked candidates. Flask's request size limit is configured to 8 MB."), sec("27.2 Model loading and reuse"), p("The model discovery helper checks available .keras files. The loader is cached with a maximum of four model instances, avoiding a fresh disk load for every prediction. This improves repeated inference but makes memory use dependent on the number and size of cached models. A production service should confirm cache behavior, startup time, and worker memory under its actual hosting configuration."), sec("27.3 Input validation"), p("The camera endpoint checks the data URL's image prefix, delimiter, and encoded length before base64 decoding. Image extensions are checked in the upload workflow and invalid model choices are rejected. These are useful application checks, but robust public deployment should also validate decoded content, pixel dimensions, MIME/content consistency, execution time, and request rates."), sec("27.4 Response design"), p("The model scores are sorted and the top five are returned. The top label is a useful interface summary, while alternatives communicate some ambiguity. Scores should be named as model scores or confidence estimates rather than guaranteed correctness probabilities unless calibration is evaluated. A future response may include a low-confidence/unknown state and a request identifier for diagnostics without storing personal images."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(28,"Browser Interface: HTML, CSS, and JavaScript"), sec("28.1 HTML structure"), p("The template defines the page structure for image upload, live camera preview, capture, retake, prediction, and result display. Semantic controls and labels help users understand which action is available at each step. The server-rendered template also presents standard form errors and prediction results."), sec("28.2 CSS and responsive layout"), p("The stylesheet provides the visual identity, responsive layout, component states, and mobile-oriented presentation. Responsive design must preserve readable labels, tappable controls, and a stable camera preview at narrow widths. A screenshot documents a particular rendering, but browser testing at multiple viewport sizes remains necessary to establish that the layout behaves consistently."), sec("28.3 JavaScript workflow"), p("JavaScript requests camera access, attaches the media stream to a visible video element, draws the chosen frame to a canvas, converts that still to an image data URL, and submits it only after the user chooses Predict. Capture and retake make the review state explicit. This is image capture followed by prediction, not continuous video inference."), sec("28.4 Error and permission states"), p("Camera access depends on user permission, supported browser APIs, and a secure context. If permission is denied, no device is available, or the page is not served from localhost/HTTPS, the user should receive a clear alternative such as uploading a photo. The camera UI should also stop media tracks when the workflow ends so that the camera indicator turns off."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(29,"Progressive Web App and Camera APIs"), sec("29.1 PWA manifest"), p("static/manifest.webmanifest describes the app name, start URL, standalone display preference, theme colors, and icons. Compatible browsers can use this metadata to offer an installable experience. Installation behavior varies by browser and platform and should be checked on actual target devices."), sec("29.2 Service worker"), p("The service worker caches selected application-shell GET resources. This can make parts of the interface available more quickly and may help the shell reopen when connectivity is limited. It does not include the saved TensorFlow model or perform local inference. A prediction still requires the browser to reach the Flask service."), sec("29.3 getUserMedia and still capture"), p("The browser camera API supplies a live MediaStream for preview. The JavaScript capture action draws one frame to a canvas and sends that image to the backend when the user requests prediction. Browser permission is controlled by the user; camera access is normally available only on HTTPS or localhost. Device orientation and camera selection can vary and merit cross-device checks."), sec("29.4 Privacy and lifecycle"), p("The current repository does not define accounts or a database for uploaded photos. A release should still explain how requests are processed and whether hosting logs include request metadata. The interface should stop the stream on retake/close/navigation as appropriate, avoid accidental repeated submissions, and expose an upload fallback."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(30,"Interface Screenshot: Upload Workflow"), p("The following project-interface preview is derived from the implemented HTML/CSS interface and presentation capture. It illustrates the upload-oriented screen and is not a screenshot of a verified live hosted service."), Spacer(1,4*mm), add_image(BUILD/"upload_screen_detail.png", 162*mm, 145*mm), p("Figure 30.1. EcoSort AI upload screen preview. The user selects an image and requests server-side classification.", "CaptionEco"), sec("30.1 Expected user sequence"), *bullet_list(["Choose a supported image file from the device.","Confirm the selected image is the intended item and is clearly visible.","Submit the image to the Flask service for prediction.","Review the top category and ranked alternatives as model suggestions.","Use local disposal guidance for the final sorting decision."]), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(31,"Interface Screenshot: Camera Capture"), p("This camera-screen preview shows the live preview and capture-oriented controls represented in the project interface. It documents the intended browser workflow, not evidence that a camera was active in the screenshot or that a particular host deployment was tested."), Spacer(1,4*mm), add_image(BUILD/"camera_screen_detail.png", 162*mm, 145*mm), p("Figure 31.1. Camera workflow interface preview. Capture first, review the still image, then explicitly request prediction.", "CaptionEco"), sec("31.1 Interaction states"), make_table([["State","What the user sees","Next action"],["Permission pending","Camera start/status feedback","Allow access or choose upload."],["Live preview","Visible camera image and capture action","Frame a single item and capture."],["Still review","Captured image replacing/overlaid on preview","Predict or retake."],["Prediction result","Top category and ranked alternatives","Review suggestion; check local rules."]],[34*mm,69*mm,59*mm]), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(32,"Dataset Sample Gallery"), p("The figure below contains one representative image selected from each class directory in the repository. These are dataset examples, not app prediction screenshots. A small gallery helps readers understand the visual range and label vocabulary, but it is not a substitute for a dataset audit or a held-out test set."), Spacer(1,5*mm), add_image(BUILD/"dataset_sample_gallery.png", 164*mm, 150*mm), p("Figure 32.1. One repository sample per configured class: battery, biological, brown glass, cardboard, clothes, green glass, metal, paper, plastic, shoes, trash, and white glass.", "CaptionEco"), sec("32.1 Visual interpretation"), p("Some categories represent material families (paper, metal, glass, plastic), while others describe object or use categories (clothes, shoes, battery) or a broad residual class (trash). These labels are not perfectly mutually exclusive descriptions of material composition. The learned target follows the dataset's folder labels; category definitions should be documented before interpreting model errors or extending the class list."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(33,"Class Distribution and Results Interpretation"), sec("33.1 Recorded counts"), p("The dataset contains 15,515 labeled images across twelve classes. Clothes represents about one third of the examples; shoes represents about 12.7%; each remaining category is below 7%. This structure explains why the training notebook computes class weights and why aggregate validation accuracy is an incomplete view of model quality."), make_table([["Group","Count","Approximate share"],["Clothes","5,325","34.3%"],["Shoes","1,977","12.7%"],["Paper","1,050","6.8%"],["Biological","985","6.3%"],["Battery","945","6.1%"],["Other seven classes","5,233","33.7%"],["Total","15,515","100.0%"]],[56*mm,40*mm,66*mm]), sec("33.2 Validation result"), p("The saved metadata records validation accuracy of 0.9468 and validation loss of 0.1720. Accuracy is the fraction of validation examples whose highest-scoring class matches the label. Loss captures the model's categorical error under the training loss definition. Neither statistic independently establishes performance on images captured by new users, and neither reveals which classes are confused."), sec("33.3 Claims boundary"), p("No result is reported for an independent test dataset because none is documented in the repository snapshot. The report also does not invent per-class precision, recall, F1, a confusion matrix, camera latency, or user-study outcomes. Such values should be added only after running and recording the corresponding evaluation protocol."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(34,"User Guide: Photo Upload"), sec("34.1 Prepare a useful image"), p("Choose a well-lit image with one item occupying a substantial part of the frame. Keep the item in focus, avoid covering its important shape, and use a background that does not visually merge with it. The app predicts from the pixels provided; it cannot read a recycling symbol or infer a local collection rule unless a separate feature is developed."), sec("34.2 Submit and read the result"), *bullet_list(["Open the EcoSort AI page and choose the upload workflow.","Select an allowed image file and verify that the preview matches the intended object.","Submit once and wait for the server response.","Read the highest-ranked category and any alternatives shown by the interface.","Treat the scores as model output, not a guarantee; check local waste guidance."]), sec("34.3 If the result is poor"), p("Retake the photo with better focus and lighting, simplify the scene, and ensure the object is large enough to recognize. If the object contains several materials or is outside the twelve classes, the classifier may still force a known category. A wrong result should be recorded with the image conditions for later evaluation, subject to the project's privacy policy."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(35,"User Guide: Camera and Troubleshooting"), sec("35.1 Camera steps"), *bullet_list(["Open the site on HTTPS or localhost and choose camera scan.","Allow camera permission when the browser prompts.","Frame one item and confirm the live preview is visible.","Capture a still image and inspect it before continuing.","Choose Predict to submit that still; choose Retake to capture again.","When finished, close the camera workflow so the media stream can stop."]), sec("35.2 Common issues"), make_table([["Symptom","Likely cause","Suggested action"],["Browser does not request camera","Insecure origin, permission already denied, or unsupported browser","Use HTTPS/localhost; review site permissions; try upload."],["Black or blank preview","Camera busy, blocked, or no device","Close other camera apps; check permissions/device; refresh."],["Prediction request rejected","Large/invalid payload or model unavailable","Try a smaller supported photo; confirm server model files."],["Unexpected category","Ambiguous image, domain shift, or model limitation","Improve framing; compare alternatives; do not treat as definitive."]],[44*mm,58*mm,60*mm]), sec("35.3 Responsible use"), p("The tool should not be used to determine safe handling of hazardous materials or to override municipal instructions. Batteries and special waste need verified collection guidance. If uncertain, do not place an item in a recycling stream solely because of a model label."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(36,"Deployment Environment and Compatibility"), sec("36.1 Configured service"), make_table([["Item","Project setting"],["Runtime","Python 3.11.9"],["Dependency installation","pip install -r requirements.txt"],["WSGI process","gunicorn --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT app:app"],["Application object","app.py:app"],["Model files","models/*.keras and models/metadata.json"],["Request size","Flask request body maximum 8 MB"],["Camera origin","HTTPS required for hosted browser camera access"]],[49*mm,113*mm]), sec("36.2 TensorFlow packaging concern"), p("The earlier deployment log showed package resolution stopping at a TensorFlow lower-bound constraint because no compatible distribution was available for the selected environment. The repository now contains explicit Python 3.11.9 pins; a deploy must be built from the latest commit containing those files. A successful local installation does not prove a hosted build will succeed, because operating system, architecture, Python patch version, package index, and memory limits also matter."), sec("36.3 Deployment verification"), p("After publishing, record the commit hash, runtime reported by the host, dependency installation log, service health response, model load outcome, and a controlled test prediction. Camera access must be checked on the public HTTPS domain and on target phone browsers. The mere presence of render.yaml is configuration evidence, not proof that the service is live."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(37,"Integration, Verification, and Traceability"), sec("37.1 End-to-end data path"), FlowDiagram(["User selects /\ncaptures image", "HTTP request\nvalidation", "RGB resize\n224 x 224", "Saved model\npredict", "Metadata map\nrank scores", "UI result"]), sec("37.2 Traceability matrix"), make_table([["Requirement","Implementation evidence","Verification status"],["Twelve-class prediction","Notebook, models/*.keras, models/metadata.json","Saved artifact/metadata present; independent test not recorded."],["Photo upload","templates/index.html and Flask root route","Code path present; formal test run not recorded."],["Camera still capture","JavaScript camera/canvas flow and /api/predict-frame","Implemented capture-then-predict flow; device matrix not recorded."],["Class mapping","metadata.json class_names","Must match model output ordering; recommend automated check."],["Mobile/PWA shell","CSS, manifest, service worker","Files present; cross-browser installation not recorded."],["Hosted execution","render.yaml, runtime.txt, .python-version","Configuration present; current public status unverified."]],[38*mm,72*mm,52*mm],font=7.4), sec("37.3 Minimum release checks"), p("A release should run a known-image smoke test, invalid-file test, missing-model test, camera permission/capture test, narrow-screen test, and hosted cold-start test. Test evidence should record software versions, image fixture identifiers, expected behavior, actual outcome, and date. Until that record exists, this report identifies these as recommended checks, not completed passes."), PageBreak()]

story += [chapter(38,"Conclusion"), p("EcoSort AI demonstrates an end-to-end image-classification prototype for waste categories. The project organizes 15,515 images into twelve class folders, applies transfer learning with MobileNetV2, and exposes inference through a Flask application. The web interface supports photo upload and a camera workflow that shows a live preview, captures a still, permits review, and predicts only after explicit user action."), p("The saved model metadata reports 94.68% validation accuracy and 0.172 validation loss. Those values are tied to the project validation split and should not be generalized to arbitrary real-world images. The repository does not provide an independent test benchmark or class-level performance report. This distinction is central to interpreting the result responsibly."), p("The principal outcome is an integrated academic prototype connecting dataset preparation, model training, inference, and an accessible browser experience. Before broader use, the work should improve dataset provenance, independent evaluation, minority-class analysis, low-confidence handling, deployment monitoring, and locally verified disposal information. Within those boundaries, the project provides a practical foundation for continued study of computer vision in waste sorting."), Spacer(1,5*mm), p("Final project outcome", "SectionEco"), p("A trained twelve-class image classifier integrated with a responsive web app for uploaded photos and user-captured camera stills, accompanied by documented validation results and a defined evaluation roadmap.", "CalloutEco"), PageBreak()]

